WEBVTT

00:00.940 --> 00:03.500
Let's see if we can stumble
our way to another

00:03.500 --> 00:05.590
logarithm property.

00:05.590 --> 00:21.090
So let's say that the log
base x of A is equal to B.

00:23.690 --> 00:37.000
That's the same thing as saying
that x to the B is equal to A.

00:37.000 --> 00:38.900
Fair enough.

00:38.900 --> 00:40.260
So what I want to
do is experiment.

00:40.260 --> 00:43.860
What happens if I multiply this
expression by another variable?

00:43.860 --> 00:44.690
Let's call it C.

00:44.690 --> 00:49.580
So I'm going to multiply both
sides of this equation times C.

00:49.580 --> 00:51.960
And I'll just switch colors
just to keep things

00:51.960 --> 00:52.630
interesting.

00:52.630 --> 00:53.740
That's not an x that's a C.

00:53.740 --> 00:55.120
I should probably just
do a dot instead.

00:55.120 --> 00:55.860
Times C.

00:55.860 --> 00:58.610
So I'm going to multiply both
sides of this equation times C.

00:58.610 --> 01:09.940
So I get C times log base x of
A is equal to-- multiply both

01:09.940 --> 01:14.130
sides of the equation--
is equal to B times C.

01:14.130 --> 01:15.250
Fair enough.

01:15.250 --> 01:17.990
I think you realize I
have not done anything

01:17.990 --> 01:19.980
profound just yet.

01:19.980 --> 01:20.580
But let's go back.

01:20.580 --> 01:22.300
We said that this is the
same thing as this.

01:22.300 --> 01:24.210
So let's experiment
with something.

01:24.210 --> 01:27.530
Let's raise this side
to the power of C.

01:27.530 --> 01:31.120
So I'm going to raise this
side to the power of C.

01:31.120 --> 01:32.500
That's a kind of caret.

01:32.500 --> 01:33.950
And when you type
exponents that's what

01:33.950 --> 01:34.630
you would use, a caret.

01:34.630 --> 01:36.160
So I'm going to raise
it to the power of C.

01:36.160 --> 01:44.320
So then, this side is x
to the B to the C power,

01:44.320 --> 01:47.140
is equal to A to the C.

01:47.140 --> 01:49.680
All I did is I raised both
sides of this equation

01:49.680 --> 01:51.430
to the Cth power.

01:51.430 --> 01:54.890
And what do we know about when
you raise something to an

01:54.890 --> 01:56.610
exponent and you raise that
whole thing to another

01:56.610 --> 01:57.710
exponent, what happens
to the exponents?

01:57.710 --> 02:00.430
Well, that's just exponent
rule and you just multiply

02:00.430 --> 02:03.640
those two exponents.

02:03.640 --> 02:12.460
This just implies that x to the
BC is equal to A to the C.

02:12.460 --> 02:13.350
What can we do now?

02:13.350 --> 02:14.390
Well, I don't know.

02:14.390 --> 02:17.920
Let's take the logarithm
of both sides.

02:17.920 --> 02:20.010
Or let's just write this--
let's not take the

02:20.010 --> 02:20.990
logarithm of both sides.

02:20.990 --> 02:23.170
Let's write this as a
logarithm expression.

02:23.170 --> 02:27.280
We know that x to the BC
is equal to A to the C.

02:27.280 --> 02:31.240
Well, that's the exact same
thing as saying that the

02:31.240 --> 02:43.400
logarithm base x of A to
the C is equal to BC.

02:43.400 --> 02:44.220
Correct?

02:44.220 --> 02:48.310
Because all I did is I rewrote
this as a logarithm expression.

02:48.310 --> 02:50.460
And I think now you
realized that something

02:50.460 --> 02:52.170
interesting has happened.

02:52.170 --> 02:58.120
That BC, well, of course, it's
the same thing as this BC.

02:58.120 --> 03:04.350
So this expression must be
equal to this expression.

03:04.350 --> 03:08.200
And I think we have another
logarithm property.

03:08.200 --> 03:11.750
That if I have some kind of a
coefficient in front of the

03:11.750 --> 03:15.740
logarithm where I'm multiplying
the logarithm, so if I have C

03:15.740 --> 03:20.820
log-- Clog base x of A,
but that's C times the

03:20.820 --> 03:22.660
logarithm base x of A.

03:22.660 --> 03:29.730
That equals the log
base x of A to the C.

03:29.730 --> 03:32.410
So you could take this
coefficient and instead make

03:32.410 --> 03:36.490
it an exponent on the term
inside the logarithm.

03:36.490 --> 03:38.850
That is another
logarithm property.

03:38.850 --> 03:41.220
So let's review what we know
so far about logarithms.

03:45.420 --> 03:54.320
We know that if I write-- let
me say-- well, let me just with

03:54.320 --> 03:55.120
the letters I've been using.

03:55.120 --> 04:04.430
C times logarithm base x of
A is equal to logarithm

04:04.430 --> 04:07.480
base x of A to the C.

04:07.480 --> 04:09.710
We know that.

04:09.710 --> 04:16.880
And we know-- we just learned
that logarithm base x of A plus

04:16.880 --> 04:23.360
logarithm base x of B is equal
to the logarithm base

04:23.360 --> 04:27.130
x of A times B.

04:27.130 --> 04:28.340
Now let me ask you a question.

04:28.340 --> 04:33.850
What happens if instead of
a positive sign here we

04:33.850 --> 04:35.530
put a negative sign?

04:35.530 --> 04:38.180
Well, you could probably figure
it out yourself but we could do

04:38.180 --> 04:40.320
that same exact proof that
we did in the beginning.

04:40.320 --> 04:45.000
But in this time we will
set it up with a negative.

04:45.000 --> 04:53.235
Let's just say that log
base x of A is equal to l.

04:53.235 --> 04:58.845
Let's say that log base
x of B is equal to m.

05:01.690 --> 05:14.330
Let's say that log base x of A
divided by B is equal to n.

05:14.330 --> 05:16.550
How can we write all of these
expressions as exponents?

05:16.550 --> 05:23.340
Well, this just says that
x to the l is equal to A.

05:23.340 --> 05:24.190
Let me switch colors.

05:24.190 --> 05:26.230
That keeps it interesting.

05:26.230 --> 05:30.840
This is just saying that x
to the m is equal to B.

05:30.840 --> 05:39.600
And this is just saying that
x to the n is equal to A/B.

05:45.000 --> 05:46.410
So what can we do here?

05:46.410 --> 05:49.660
Well what's another
way of writing A/B?

05:49.660 --> 05:53.580
Well, that's just the same
thing as writing x to the l

05:53.580 --> 05:59.250
because that's A,
over x to the m.

05:59.250 --> 05:59.770
That's B.

06:03.290 --> 06:07.980
And this we know from our
exponent rules-- this could

06:07.980 --> 06:14.800
also be written as x to the
l, x to the negative m.

06:14.800 --> 06:20.780
Or that also equals
x to the l minus m.

06:20.780 --> 06:21.490
So what do we know?

06:21.490 --> 06:25.020
We know that x to the n is
equal to x to the l minus m.

06:31.140 --> 06:32.070
Those equal each other.

06:32.070 --> 06:33.900
I just made a big
equal chain here.

06:33.900 --> 06:40.226
So we know that n is
equal to l minus m.

06:40.226 --> 06:42.340
Well, what does that do for us?

06:42.340 --> 06:44.340
Well, what's another
way of writing n?

06:44.340 --> 06:47.370
I'm going to do it up here
because I think we have

06:47.370 --> 06:50.920
stumbled upon another
logarithm rule.

06:50.920 --> 06:52.660
What's another way
of writing n?

06:52.660 --> 06:55.160
Well, I did it right here.

06:55.160 --> 06:57.690
This is another
way of writing n.

06:57.690 --> 07:07.130
So logarithm base x of
A/B-- this is an x over

07:07.130 --> 07:09.610
here-- is equal to l.

07:09.610 --> 07:12.550
l is this right here.

07:12.550 --> 07:14.640
Log base x of A is equal to l.

07:14.640 --> 07:22.250
The log base x of A minus m.

07:22.250 --> 07:24.300
I wrote m right here.

07:24.300 --> 07:26.390
That's log base x of B.

07:30.430 --> 07:31.910
There you go.

07:31.910 --> 07:33.060
I probably didn't
have to prove it.

07:33.060 --> 07:37.085
You could've probably tried
it out with dividing

07:37.085 --> 07:38.310
it, but whatever.

07:38.310 --> 07:46.060
But you know are hopefully
satisfied that we have this new

07:46.060 --> 07:49.080
logarithm property right there.

07:49.080 --> 07:52.240
Now I have one more logarithm
property to show you, but I

07:52.240 --> 07:54.100
don't think I have time to
show it in this video.

07:54.100 --> 07:56.190
So I will do it in
the next video.

07:56.190 --> 07:57.811
I'll see you soon.
